/* Original chapter briefs only. Public titles. No quoted book text. */

static const Chapter SPIRIT[] = {
    { "The Kahuna",
      "Opens the book with the Hawaiian specialist healer as a working model, not a museum piece. The authors argue that a person is more than a body plus a diagnosis, and that traditional training treated illness as a break in relationship as much as a tissue problem. Western readers are invited to keep clinics and labs, and still learn a map of souls, spirits, and sacred space. This first Kahuna chapter sets that frame before any technique." },
    { "The Soul Cluster",
      "Hawaiian thought, as the authors present it, does not treat a human as one indivisible self. Several soul aspects travel together and can fall out of step. Healing work starts by noticing which part of that cluster is dim, missing, or overbearing. The chapter is a map of personhood, not a list of symptoms." },
    { "The Spirit Soul",
      "The spirit soul is the immortal, ancestral aspect that links a life to something older than personality. It is the part that can meet helping spirits and carry a sense of destiny or calling. When it is unheard, people often feel empty even if their body is well. The brief here is about that higher, lasting thread in the cluster." },
    { "The Body Soul",
      "The body soul is the animal, sensory, instinctive life in the flesh. It knows hunger, threat, pleasure, and the will to stay alive. Shock and chronic fear can drive it into hiding or into frantic overwork. Healing, in this model, has to speak to that embodied soul, not only to ideas." },
    { "The Mental Soul",
      "The mental soul is the narrator: identity, plans, self-talk, and the story a person tells about who they are. It is useful and also the part most likely to freeze into a rigid script. Illness can follow when the mental soul refuses what the other souls already know. The chapter asks for a more flexible mind-soul, not a war on thinking." },
    { "The Human Spirit",
      "Here the cluster is treated as one living spirit in motion rather than three separate theories. Health is cooperation among the souls; sickness is a civil war inside the person. The authors point toward a felt sense of being inhabited and accompanied. Spirit, in this usage, is a working whole." },
    { "The Physical Body and the Energy Body",
      "Dense tissue and the subtle field around it are read as two views of the same life. Pain, fatigue, and mood can show up in the energy body before a scan catches them, or linger there after a physical fix. Practitioners are asked to look at both without pretending one replaces the other. Care means treating the field and the flesh as partners." },
    { "Levels of Reality",
      "Ordinary waking life is only one working layer. Dream, vision, and the imaginal realms are treated as real places where causes and helpers can be met. The long middle of this chapter is a ladder of those levels, not a missing title. Diagnosis later in the book depends on knowing which level a problem actually lives on." },
    { "Spirit Medicine",
      "Names the practice itself: healing that works through spirit contact, ceremony, and inner travel. It is presented as an old, cross-cultural craft rather than a new brand. The point is a method you can train, not a miracle you wait for. Later chapters unpack the tools this title announces." },
    { "The Master Healer in the Imaginal Realms",
      "An inner or helping healer can be met in visionary space and asked to work with you. The authors treat that figure as a colleague, not a metaphor you invent for comfort. Relationship, courtesy, and repeated visits matter more than a single dramatic vision. This is the clinic staff of the unseen side." },
    { "Spirit Medicine and Physical Medicine",
      "The book refuses a false choice between shamanic work and hospitals. Infections, fractures, and emergencies still belong to physical medicine. Spirit work addresses meaning, soul loss, intrusion, and the field around the diagnosis. Used together they cover more of a person than either can alone." },
    { "Disharmony",
      "Illness is framed as a break in harmony: inside the soul cluster, with other people, with land, or with the sacred. That is a cause, not only a mood. Restoring relationship can change the course of a complaint that chemicals never quite reach. The chapter is about finding the broken link." },
    { "Fear",
      "Fear is treated as a classic generator of contraction, isolation, and sickness. It can chase soul parts away and invite other trouble in. The work is not to shame fear but to meet it so it stops running the house. Courage here is a healing skill, not a pose." },
    { "Soul Loss",
      "After shock, grief, or violation, a piece of vitality can split off and not come home. People describe numbness, watching life from outside, or a childhood that never quite returned. Retrieval is later; this chapter is the diagnosis of absence. You cannot treat what you still call laziness or mood." },
    { "Illness Intrusions",
      "Unwanted energetic or spirit influences can lodge in a person and act like a foreign object. The model is closer to extraction than to talk therapy. Finding what does not belong is the first half of the job; removing it without drama is the second. The chapter teaches that some suffering is an occupant, not a trait." },
    { "Power Augmentation",
      "Healing is not only subtraction. A drained person also needs power returned: allies, song, protection, and a stronger field. Traditional work often added power after cleaning a wound. This chapter is the refill, not the mop." },
    { "Diagnosis",
      "How a spirit-medicine practitioner reads a case: journey, sensation, inner sight, and questions to helpers. The goal is to locate soul loss, intrusion, disharmony, or ordinary medical need. Guessing from a textbook list is not the method. Diagnosis is a conversation with the problem on its own level." },
    { "Extraction",
      "Once an intrusion is found, it is taken out and disposed of with care. The chapter walks the ethics and the craft: consent, protection, and not taking the debris home. Extraction without later power-work can leave a hole. This is surgery of the field, done as ceremony." },
    { "Soul Retrieval",
      "Lost soul-parts are located, invited, and helped to stay. Coming home can feel tender, young, or angry, and the everyday life has to make room. The authors treat retrieval as relationship repair, not a trophy. Integration is part of the healing, not an afterthought." },
    { "Healing in the Garden",
      "The sacred garden is introduced as the authors' main imaginal clinic. Work that might be done at a drum or an altar can also be done as tending a living inner landscape. Plants, paths, and the beings who live there become part of treatment. Later garden chapters teach the how; this one names the place." },
    { "How to Use the Compact Disc",
      "A titled how-to for the bundled drumming and rattling recording. It tells the reader when to play it, how to sit or lie down, and how the tracks support the journeys in the rest of the book. It is a published contents entry, not liner-note scrap. The disc is a tool, not optional decoration." },
    { "Protection",
      "Journeying without a boundary is treated as careless. Allies, closing, and knowing who is allowed in the space come before adventure. The chapter is practical: how not to bring stray influences back into ordinary life. Safety is part of the medicine." },
    { "The Journey",
      "The classic shamanic journey method used throughout the book: a repeated rhythm, an intention, and a return. Readers are shown how to travel and come back rather than drift. This is the vehicle for diagnosis, retrieval, and meeting helpers. Technique first, then the garden work that follows." },
    { "The Sacred Garden",
      "Finding your own inner landscape and recognizing it as a real working place. First visits are about arriving, looking around, and greeting whoever is already there. The garden is personal, not a stock visualization. It becomes the home ground for later rituals." },
    { "Gardenwork",
      "Ongoing care of that place: clearing wreckage, planting, asking permission, and keeping appointments with its residents. A neglected garden mirrors a neglected inner life. The chapter treats tending as practice, not a one-time tour. Small regular work beats a rare spectacular visit." },
    { "The Place of Power",
      "Inside or beside the garden is a site that restores strength when you stand there. People use it before hard conversations, after loss, or when the field feels thin. It is a battery and a refuge, not an escape hatch from ordinary duty. The chapter teaches how to find and return to it." },
    { "True Magic",
      "Magic, as used here, means disciplined relationship with unseen helpers and natural law, not stage tricks. Intention, timing, and humility matter more than collecting techniques. The authors separate showmanship from work that actually changes a life. Power without courtesy is not the path they are teaching." },
    { "A Healing Ritual in the Garden",
      "A complete ritual sequence you can walk in imaginal space: arrive, state the need, ask, receive, give thanks, and close. It gathers tools from earlier chapters into one sitting. The point is a repeatable ceremony, not a unique peak experience. Readers are meant to adapt the bones, not recite a script." },
    { "The SpiritMedicine Dance",
      "Movement is offered as a way to embody healing that the mind already understands. Dance, rattle, and breath finish work that sitting still can leave half-done. The chapter treats the body as a partner that must move the change into muscle and mood. Spirit medicine here is kinetic." },
    { "A Journey to the Spirit of Illness",
      "Instead of only fighting a disease, the practitioner asks the illness what it wants and what must change. The spirit of a complaint can be a messenger, a trespasser, or both. Listening first can prevent a war that leaves the real cause untouched. This journey is a negotiation, not a hunt." },
    { "A Healing Meditation",
      "A quieter, seated form of the same work for people who do not want a full drum journey every time. Stillness, breath, and inner garden imagery carry the session. It is a published contents chapter, not an appendix exercise. Meditation here is spirit medicine at a lower volume." },
    { "Spirit Medicine in Groups",
      "How the method scales to a circle: shared rhythm, consent, and not dumping one person's debris on the room. Group field can amplify healing and also spread sloppiness. Roles, closing, and aftercare keep a ceremony from becoming theatre. The chapter is about doing this with other people, safely." },
    { "Healing Long Distance",
      "Working for someone who is not in the room, with permission and care. Distance is less important than relationship and a clean intention. The authors treat remote work as real and also as easy to abuse. Ethics sit beside the technique." },
    { "Ancestral Lineage Healing",
      "Gifts and wounds travel family lines. Meeting ancestors as allies can loosen patterns that look personal but are inherited. The work is respect and sorting, not blaming the dead. A healthier lineage field often changes the living without a courtroom scene." },
    { "The Medicinemaker",
      "What it means to take up the healer's craft as a way of life rather than a weekend hobby. Character, apprenticeship, and service matter more than collecting journeys. The title nods to the author's earlier path without turning this chapter into memoir. A medicinemaker is someone the community can actually send a sick person to." },
    { "The Kahuna",
      "The closing Kahuna chapter returns to the opening figure with the whole map now in hand. The specialist healer is no longer an exotic introduction; they are the kind of person the book has been training the reader to respect and, in a modest way, to emulate. The authors leave a completed circuit rather than a cliffhanger. Front and back Kahuna titles are two different published chapters." }
};

static const Chapter SUBTLE[] = {
    { "Don't Try",
      "The opening move is against forced positivity and the demand to care about everything. Attention is limited, so the real skill is choosing a few things worth caring about and dropping the rest. Trying to be extraordinary at all times is treated as a trap, not a virtue. The chapter sets a blunt, selective stance for the rest of the book." },
    { "Happiness Is a Problem",
      "A good life is not a life without problems. Happiness shows up while you work on problems you have accepted as yours. Emotions are signals, not bosses, and the useful question is which struggle you are willing to keep. Avoiding all discomfort just trades one misery for a duller one." },
    { "You Are Not Special",
      "Entitlement and the cult of being exceptional make ordinary days feel like failure. Most lives are statistically average, and that can still be a decent life. The hunger to be uniquely broken or uniquely gifted is treated as the same habit. Dropping the specialness story frees energy for actual work and actual people." },
    { "The Value of Suffering",
      "Pain is given; values decide which pain is useful. Status, constant pleasure, and always being right are treated as poor metrics. Better values look like honesty, curiosity, and responsibility because they survive contact with other people. The chapter is a filter for what is worth suffering over." },
    { "You Are Always Choosing",
      "You do not always choose what happens. You always choose the next response. Fault and responsibility are pulled apart so that an unfair past does not cancel today's move. Victim stories are acknowledged as sometimes true and still insufficient as a life plan. Agency is the habit of picking the next card you can actually play." },
    { "You're Wrong About Everything (But So Am I)",
      "Beliefs are provisional sketches, not finished maps. Certainty often hides fear of being changed by new facts. The chapter asks for a working humility: stay a little unsure on purpose so you can update. Being wrong is information. Being un-updateable is the real failure." },
    { "Failure Is the Way Forward",
      "Improvement is a loop of doing, failing, feeling it, and doing the next small thing. Waiting to feel ready is treated as a stall. Action teaches faster than self-concept work done in a chair. The point is a bias toward motion, not a romance with collapse." },
    { "The Importance of Saying No",
      "A life gets its shape from rejection as much as from appetite. Boundaries make trust possible; endless yeses make it cheap. Freedom, in this chapter, comes from fewer and deeper commitments, not from keeping every door open. Saying no is how values become visible to other people." },
    { "...And Then You Die",
      "Mortality is used as a clarifier, not a dare. Facing the fact that the story ends shrinks entitlement and the need to be impressive. What remains is the handful of values you are still willing to stand on. The last numbered chapter treats death as the light that makes ordinary chosen cares enough." }
};

static const Chapter POWER[] = {
    { "Preface",
      "Greene's published front-matter chapter argues that nobody enjoys feeling helpless, yet modern manners punish obvious hunger for power. The useful stance is outward fairness and inward strategy: learn the old court games without announcing that you are playing them. History is offered as a catalog of people who rose or fell by these patterns. The preface is a real contents entry, then the forty-eight laws begin." },
    { "Law 1  Never Outshine the Master",
      "People above you need to feel securely superior. Displaying more talent than they can comfortably own often breeds fear, not promotion. The practical move is to make their brilliance more visible than yours until you no longer need their cover. Competence still matters; timing and presentation decide whether it helps you or hunts you." },
    { "Law 2  Never Put Too Much Trust in Friends, Learn How to Use Enemies",
      "Friends can sour into envy and entitlement because intimacy blurs the ledger. A former opponent who comes over has something to prove and often works cleaner. Hire for skill and aligned interest, not for comfort. Keep affection and power appointments on separate books." },
    { "Law 3  Conceal Your Intentions",
      "A visible plan is a target. Smoke, side paths, and decoy desires keep rivals guessing until the move is already done. Talking through every ambition invites blockers. Silence about the real aim is a tool, not a personality trait." },
    { "Law 4  Always Say Less Than Necessary",
      "Extra words create extra openings. Short, calm speech reads as control; chatter reads as need. Once something is said, you no longer own it. The chapter treats restraint as a power skill, including knowing when a sentence should end." },
    { "Law 5  So Much Depends on Reputation -- Guard It With Your Life",
      "Reputation does work ahead of you and is expensive to rebuild. Guard it, and if you must fight, fight the story that would stain it. Other people's names can be used carefully; your own is the asset you cannot replace. A cracked reputation makes every later law harder." },
    { "Law 6  Court Attention at All Costs",
      "Obscurity is a kind of defeat. Being seen, even with a managed controversy, keeps you in the game. The warning is to own the spectacle rather than let others define it. Attention is oxygen; vanishing is how careers quietly end." },
    { "Law 7  Get Others to Do the Work for You, but Always Take the Credit",
      "Use other people's labor, research, and momentum, then attach the result to your name. History remembers the person who framed the win, not every hand that built it. The ugly version is theft; the court version is patronage and editing. Either way, credit is treated as a scarce resource you should not donate." },
    { "Law 8  Make Other People Come to You -- Use Bait if Necessary",
      "Whoever travels to the meeting has already spent more. Draw others onto your ground with offers they cannot ignore. Fighting on their timetable and turf surrenders initiative. Patience plus bait beats chasing." },
    { "Law 9  Win Through Your Actions, Never Through Argument",
      "Winning a debate often loses the person. Demonstration settles more than logic because people protect their pride. Show the result and let the argument die of neglect. Words are for framing; proof is for closing." },
    { "Law 10  Infection: Avoid the Unhappy and Unlucky",
      "Chronic misery and chaos spread through association. Some people are not projects; they are weather systems. Distance is presented as hygiene, not cruelty. Choose companions whose patterns you can afford to catch." },
    { "Law 11  Learn to Keep People Dependent on You",
      "Freedom grows when others need you and shrinks when they can replace you. Teach enough to be valued, not enough to be optional. Unique access, skill, or relationship is a leash that runs both ways. Independence is safest when you are still the bottleneck." },
    { "Law 12  Use Selective Honesty and Generosity to Disarm Your Victim",
      "One sincere gift can open a locked door that force cannot. Timed honesty lowers suspicion so later moves land cleanly. The chapter is about a tool, not a conversion to virtue. Generosity is most powerful when it is rare and visible." },
    { "Law 13  When Asking for Help, Appeal to People's Self-Interest, Never to Their Mercy or Gratitude",
      "People help faster when they can see their own gain. Past favors and pity are weak currency. State the benefit to them in language they already use. Need dressed as opportunity gets further than need dressed as debt." },
    { "Law 14  Pose as a Friend, Work as a Spy",
      "Information is power, and casual intimacy is how it leaks. Listen more than you perform. What people reveal in comfort is worth more than what they announce in meetings. The smile is cover for a notebook, not a substitute for one." },
    { "Law 15  Crush Your Enemy Totally",
      "A wounded rival has a reason to return. Half-victories create future wars. If you choose a fight, end the ability to retaliate, not just the scene. Mercy that leaves a capable enemy is treated as a delay, not a virtue." },
    { "Law 16  Use Absence to Increase Respect and Honor",
      "Too much presence cheapens you. Withdrawal, used sparingly, raises price and mystery. The same person in every room becomes furniture. Leave before they are finished wanting you." },
    { "Law 17  Keep Others in Suspended Terror: Cultivate an Air of Unpredictability",
      "A readable pattern is a handle. Unpredictable timing and mood force others to hesitate and over-prepare. You do not need chaos; you need unreadability. People give more space to someone they cannot forecast." },
    { "Law 18  Do Not Build Fortresses to Protect Yourself -- Isolation Is Dangerous",
      "Walls keep news out as well as danger. Isolated leaders get surprised. Stay in the flow of people, gossip, and shifting alliances even when it feels safer to hide. Connection is intelligence; a fortress is a blindfold." },
    { "Law 19  Know Who You're Dealing With -- Do Not Offend the Wrong Person",
      "People are not interchangeable. Some swallow insult; some collect it for years. Study the temperament before the tactic. A move that works on one character is suicide on another." },
    { "Law 20  Do Not Commit to Anyone",
      "Early, public allegiance shrinks options. Stay desirable to both sides long enough to see the real board. Commitment is a card you play late, not a personality. Neutrality here is leverage, not indecision." },
    { "Law 21  Play a Sucker to Catch a Sucker -- Seem Dumber Than Your Mark",
      "Letting others feel smarter lowers their guard. Displayed intelligence invites counter-moves; displayed limits invite sloppiness. Ask questions you already know the answers to. Pride in looking clever is expensive." },
    { "Law 22  Use the Surrender Tactic: Transform Weakness into Power",
      "Open surrender can stop a blow you cannot yet return. Yielding buys time, information, and a later angle. It is a tactic, not a character change. The person who can bow without breaking often chooses the next field." },
    { "Law 23  Concentrate Your Forces",
      "Scattered effort looks busy and dies. Put weight on the one point that actually moves the situation. Half-presence in many arenas is a gift to specialists. Intensity beats coverage." },
    { "Law 24  Play the Perfect Courtier",
      "Courts, offices, and crews all run on manners that are not written down. Flatter without looking like a flatterer, carry news, and never make the powerful feel watched. Social grace is treated as a combat skill. Clumsy honesty at the wrong table is a self-own." },
    { "Law 25  Re-Create Yourself",
      "Do not wait for the world to assign you a role. Invent a figure people can see and remember, then inhabit it. Past versions can be retired in public. Identity, used this way, is costume and strategy." },
    { "Law 26  Keep Your Hands Clean",
      "Someone else should carry the ugly job when it has to be done. Distance from the mess preserves the face you still need. Scapegoats and cat's-paws are the dark form of delegation. The law is about stain, not about innocence." },
    { "Law 27  Play on People's Need to Believe to Create a Cultlike Following",
      "People want a story that makes them feel chosen. A clear myth, a little theatre, and a promise of transformation can gather a following faster than policy. The risk is becoming prisoner of the myth you sold. Belief is fuel; it is also a leash." },
    { "Law 28  Enter Action with Boldness",
      "Hesitation reads as weakness and invites pushback. Bold entry can create the confidence it pretends to have. Timid starts leak information and courage. If you are going to move, move as if the outcome were already decided." },
    { "Law 29  Plan All the Way to the End",
      "Most plans die in the middle because nobody mapped the last mile. Think through aftermath, enemies created, and the day after the win. Ending conditions belong in the first draft. Improvisation is for gaps, not for the destination." },
    { "Law 30  Make Your Accomplishments Seem Effortless",
      "Visible strain makes people discount the result. Hide the rehearsal; show the landing. Effortlessness is a performance that protects mystery and status. Sweat belongs offstage." },
    { "Law 31  Control the Options: Get Others to Play with the Cards You Deal",
      "A choice between your two preferred outcomes is not freedom for them. Frame the menu so every pick serves you. People fight harder against a single demand than against a set they think they chose. Options are a cage with nice wallpaper." },
    { "Law 32  Play to People's Fantasies",
      "Harsh reality has a small audience. Offer a story people already want to be true and they will help you carry it. Fantasy outsells audit. The chapter is about appetite, not about lying for sport." },
    { "Law 33  Discover Each Man's Thumbscrew",
      "Everyone has a lever: vanity, fear, a secret, a hunger to be seen. Find it before you need it. Generic pressure wastes motion. Specific weakness, used quietly, moves a person further than force." },
    { "Law 34  Be Royal in Your Own Fashion: Act Like a King to Be Treated Like One",
      "People take their cue from how you treat yourself. Smallness invites small treatment. Carry yourself as if the room already agrees, without cheap swagger. Dignity is a signal that others often copy." },
    { "Law 35  Master the Art of Timing",
      "The right move at the wrong hour is the wrong move. Wait, strike, or vanish according to the clock of the situation, not your impatience. Time is an ally only if you can stand still. Rushing is how good positions are donated." },
    { "Law 36  Disdain Things You Cannot Have: Ignoring Them Is the Best Revenge",
      "Chasing what you cannot reach advertises lack. Public indifference can shrink the prize and spare you the chase. Attention is a gift; withhold it from what would cheapen you. Some victories are just refused obsessions." },
    { "Law 37  Create Compelling Spectacles",
      "Images land faster than arguments. Ceremony, symbols, and staged moments can move a crowd that a memo cannot. Power needs theatre because people remember what they saw. Make the picture, then let talk follow." },
    { "Law 38  Think as You Like but Behave Like Others",
      "Private dissent is cheap; public oddness is costly. Blend in enough to keep access, and keep your real views for rooms that can hold them. Difference flaunted too early becomes a target. Survival first, distinction later." },
    { "Law 39  Stir Up Waters to Catch Fish",
      "Calm people think. Agitated people leak and err. A managed disturbance can make rivals sloppy while you stay still. Anger is useful when it is theirs, not yours." },
    { "Law 40  Despise the Free Lunch",
      "What costs nothing often costs independence. Paid-for things keep the ledger clean; gifts create hooks. Prefer fair exchange over favors you cannot price. Cheap things are rarely free in a power game." },
    { "Law 41  Avoid Stepping into a Great Man's Shoes",
      "Inheriting a legend sets you up for comparison you cannot win. Change the terms, the style, or the field so you are not a lesser reprint. Successors who only maintain become footnotes. Make a new shadow instead of standing in one." },
    { "Law 42  Strike the Shepherd and the Sheep Will Scatter",
      "Groups hold together around a person, not a slogan. Remove or isolate that center and the rest often folds. Fighting every follower is a waste. Find the one will that organizes the rest." },
    { "Law 43  Work on the Hearts and Minds of Others",
      "Force gets compliance that expires. Feeling and belief last longer. Seduce, include, and give people a story in which helping you is their idea. Occupied hearts need fewer guards." },
    { "Law 44  Disarm and Infuriate with the Mirror Effect",
      "Copying a person's manner, values, or moves can charm or unnerve them. A mirror denies them a clear enemy and can bait a rash response. Used well it is camouflage; used as mockery it is a weapon. Either way they cannot easily read your difference." },
    { "Law 45  Preach the Need for Change, but Never Reform Too Much at Once",
      "People like the idea of change and hate the feeling of it. Announce renewal, then move in steps that look like tradition. Sudden overhaul creates rebels from yesterday's friends. Pace is part of the reform." },
    { "Law 46  Never Appear Too Perfect",
      "Flawlessness breeds silent envy. A managed, human defect makes you safer to be around. Hide the real weaknesses; display a harmless one. People attack what they cannot forgive themselves for lacking." },
    { "Law 47  Do Not Go Past the Mark You Aimed For; In Victory, Learn When to Stop",
      "The win is a dangerous high. Pushing past the planned end creates new enemies and wastes the gain. Set the mark, hit it, stop. Momentum without a brake is how victors become the next cautionary tale." },
    { "Law 48  Assume Formlessness",
      "A fixed shape is easy to hit. Stay adaptable, unnamed, and hard to pin to one plan. Formlessness is not chaos; it is refusing to be a target. The last law tells you to keep moving so no single counter-strategy can finish you." }
};

